commit 779417b06cc636edd6a5c161b722951b3a9fb97b
Author: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date:   Sat Sep 19 16:23:06 2026 +0000

    build(db): initialise Alembic with the frozen schema as revision 0001
    
    The groundwork Slice 2 cannot proceed without. It changes no domain model,
    workflow, permission or matching rule, so it needed no approval; Slice 2
    itself remains blocked on D7 and on instruction to begin.
    
    R14.4 says the frozen schema IS the initial migration and that Alembic must
    not recreate it from models. Revision 0001 therefore applies
    docs/handoff/04_DATABASE/schema_v0.2.3.sql verbatim, as bytes read from the
    vendored package. It transcribes nothing, so there is no second copy of the
    baseline to drift.
    
    What is mechanically prevented, each with a test:
    
      * the revision verifies the file's SHA-256 BEFORE executing it, so editing
        the vendored schema cannot become an approved schema change by accident;
      * the declared digest is asserted equal to the file's, so the guard cannot
        be compared against a stale constant;
      * target_metadata is None, so Alembic refuses --autogenerate outright —
        with no declarative metadata, autogenerate would compare "nothing" to 48
        tables and emit a migration dropping the baseline;
      * exactly one head;
      * downgrade() from the baseline raises: dropping it is reset_db.sh's job,
        and that script refuses to run against a production-looking name.
    
    The test that carries the weight is different in kind: it compares the
    MIGRATED catalog against STATIC_AUDIT_RESULTS_v0.2.3.json, which counts the
    same objects by parsing the SQL text. Two derivations, different means,
    different representations — agreement across 48 tables, 54 enum types, 17
    functions, 37 triggers and 131 foreign keys is evidence. A migration that ran
    but built a partial schema passes "did it run" and fails this.
    
    reset_db.sh now STAMPS the initial revision, which is R14.4's actual word.
    Without it every development database carries no alembic_version, and the next
    `upgrade head` applies the baseline on top of itself and dies on the first
    duplicate object. That trap is closed and pinned: removing the stamp makes two
    tests fail, which was verified by doing it.
    
    One thing removed rather than kept: an env.py hook meant to refuse autogenerate
    with a fuller message. Alembic rejects the command before that hook is reached,
    so it was unreachable — a check reading as a guarantee while providing none.
    The test now pins Alembic's real refusal, and a companion test pins
    `target_metadata = None` as the reason, because a deliberate absence is exactly
    what a later helpful edit restores.
    
    Verification: 329 tests pass (15 new); 90 rules evidenced, 0 UNPROVEN;
    PostgreSQL Execution Gate 7/7 PASS (70/70); dev reset + fixture re-load
    idempotent; OpenAPI drift, version consistency and handoff integrity (36/36)
    all PASS.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
